.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt EPPCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm eppctl
.Nd display and set the CPU Energy/Performance Preference
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar cpuid
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl s Ar value
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility displays and sets the CPU Energy/Performance Preference.
It uses the sysctl variables provided by
.Xr hwpstate_intel 4
on Intel CPUs with HWP and by hwpstate_amd
on AMD CPUs with CPPC.
Without options,
.Nm
prints the current value for every CPU.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width ".Fl s Ar value"
.It Fl c Ar cpuid
Work on the CPU numbered
.Ar cpuid
only, instead of on all CPUs.
CPUs are numbered from 0.
This option can be given more than once to select several CPUs.
.It Fl h
Print a help message.
.It Fl s Ar value
Set the Energy/Performance Preference of the selected CPUs to
.Ar value ,
and print the old and new values:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "255" -compact
.It Li 0
most performant
.It Li 255
most energy efficient
.El
.Pp
If any CPU cannot be updated, the CPUs already changed
are restored to their previous values.
Root privileges are required.
.El
.Pp
On
.Fx 16.0
and later,
.Ar value
is the raw hardware setting, from 0 to 255.
Earlier releases use a percentage and accept only 0 to 100.
If the kernel rejects a value,
.Nm
reports the error and changes nothing.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print the value for every CPU:
.Pp
.Dl $ eppctl
.Pp
Print the value for CPU 2 only:
.Pp
.Dl $ eppctl -c 2
.Pp
Set every CPU to the most performant state:
.Pp
.Dl # eppctl -s 0
.Pp
Set CPUs 0 and 1 to a balanced value:
.Pp
.Dl # eppctl -c 0 -c 1 -s 128
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hwpstate_intel 4 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility is not a part of the
.Fx
distribution yet.
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
utility was implemented by
.An Sergey A. Osokin Aq Mt osa@FreeBSD.org
.Sh CAVEATS
By default,
.Xr hwpstate_intel 4
uses package-level control, where all CPUs of a package share one setting.
(It is turned off on hybrid CPUs and by the
.Va machdep.hwpstate_pkg_ctrl
loader tunable.)
Setting a value with
.Fl c
then changes it for the whole package, and the value shown for the other CPUs
of that package can keep its old setting.
